 | P7-09 Team mode (ADR-0049) | in progress | T1a done: `users` table (schema 5), roles `owner`/`member`/`viewer`, sessions carry their person (a session with none is the owner's, so a one-person hub is unchanged), `agora hub user add NAME [--role] | list | role ID ROLE | remove ID | login ID`; the hub asks for the role on every command (a demotion or removal applies to the open connection at once) and stops a viewer's commands that change anything with `auth.forbidden` (`action` = the command's schema name), before any node hears of them (`agora_hub::users::viewer_may` lists what a viewer may still do; a new command is refused to viewers until listed). No schema or error-code change. T2a done: an append-only `audit` table (schema 6; triggers refuse deletes and any edit but the one-time outcome of a `sent` row) with who (person id, `owner` for the owner), what (the command's schema name, `sign_in`, `user.add|role|remove`), where (node) and the outcome (`ok` or the error code; `sent` while a command has no answer); every routed command is recorded when it is sent and finished when the node answers, a refused one at once, sign-ins and changes to the team too; `agora hub audit export [--since MS] [--who ID]` writes JSON lines. Terminal bytes and message bodies are not in it. T1b done: a member or viewer sees and works in only the projects they were granted (`agora hub user grant|revoke ID NODE/PROJECT`, schema 7; an owner needs none; a member with no grant sees nothing). `agora_hub::scope::Scope` follows the event stream a client gets, remembers which project each agent, worktree, terminal and approval belongs to, and decides for every event and every command whether it is theirs; a restricted client always gets a snapshot cut to its projects (whatever position it asks to replay from) and then only its own events, and the hub refuses (`auth.forbidden`) any command that does not name something in a granted project, including everything about a whole machine (`ProjectList`, `AgentList`, `NodePolicyGet`, `TerminalOpen`, grants, usage). A machine shows up only if it hosts a granted project. A change of role or grants ends the open connection and the client reconnects into what it may now see. Hidden on purpose: terminals that belong to no agent, the shared board of a project that spans machines (tasks), messages from agents the person cannot see. Tested with plain event sequences in `scope.rs` and end to end in `crates/agora-hub/tests/server.rs`. T2b done: the owner reads the trail in the web app (`#/audit`, "Activity" in the command palette): `GET /api/audit?since=&who=&limit=` with the session token, owner only (401 without a live session, 403 for a member or viewer, 400 for a query it does not understand), newest first, filterable by person; actions are named in words from `locales/{en,tr}/web.ftl` (an action this build has no words for shows as the hub named it). Screenshots in `docs/screenshots/audit-*.png`. Each row names the session that made it by a short id (schema 10, L16), the sign-in row included. T1c done (node side): `device.<id>.projects = a, b` in the node's `policy` (`agora_node::limits::DeviceLimits`): a limited trusted device can sign grants only scoped `project:X` or `agent:A` of its projects (never `node`; `auth.forbidden` `grant.create`) and can answer approvals only for agents of its projects (`auth.forbidden` `approval.resolve`, nothing consumed); an unnamed device is limited by nothing; a named one with no projects acts in none. No schema change. With limited devices and no `signed_commands` line, the node requires a grant for every command that changes something (`SignedCommands::team_default`), so the limits bind them; whole-machine commands stay the owner's. Not done: approval decisions as their own rows (they are `ApprovalResolve` commands today), devices owned by a user, Postgres (T3), the shared board for members |
